The optional CLI shares Rivet's Lua core with Neovim and supports all six bundled languages. This quick start uses C++ with conventional headers. For another language, follow [Python](/docs/first-python-project/), [Rust](/docs/first-rust-project/), [SwiftPM](/docs/first-swift-project/), [C](/docs/first-c-project/), or [Lua](/docs/first-lua-project/). First [download the source archive or obtain a checkout](/docs/start-here/#local-development).

## Check the tools

The CLI needs Lua 5.1 or newer, or LuaJIT. The editor interface needs Neovim 0.11 or newer. No LuaRocks packages are required. Lua runs a script through its interpreter; LuaJIT is an alternative runtime, not a requirement for making the CLI executable.

For the default C++ project, install CMake 3.30 or newer, a compiler that accepts C++26, and Ninja 1.11 or newer. Named modules also need compatible compiler dependency scanning. Homebrew LLVM supplies `clang++` and `clang-scan-deps` on macOS. `clang-format` provides actual formatting previews and formats generated sources. `just` is optional.

From the Rivet checkout:

```sh
./bin/rivet doctor
```

The launcher uses `lua` by default. With LuaJIT, run `RIVET_LUA=luajit ./bin/rivet doctor`.

Doctor reports tool availability. A successful configure and build verifies the selected project's actual compiler requirements. Rivet does not install or upgrade tools.

## Create a first project

Put the checkout's `bin` directory on your `PATH`, or use its full path instead of `rivet`:

```sh
rivet new ./engine \
  --name engine --standard 26 --modules false --std-modules false \
  --layout feature --style LLVM --git true --preview
```

Read the displayed file plan. Run the same command without `--preview` to create it, then build and test:

```sh
rivet build --root ./engine
rivet test --root ./engine
rivet run --root ./engine
```

An explicit compiler can be selected with `--compiler /path/to/clang++`. Its path is written to ignored `CMakeUserPresets.json`, keeping shared presets portable.

For a terminal questionnaire, use `rivet new PATH --interactive`. For the editor wizard, follow [Neovim workflow](/docs/neovim/) and run `:RivetNew`.

## Use the current folder

From a folder you want to turn into a project:

```sh
mkdir engine
cd engine
rivet new . --git true --preview
```

The project name defaults to `engine`, with no extra subfolder. Review it, then rerun without `--preview`. Use `--name` to override the inferred name. Spaces and punctuation become underscores; leading digits and reserved C++ names get a `project_` prefix. Subsequent `rivet build`, `rivet test`, and `rivet run` commands find the project from this folder.

`--git true` initializes a repository after the files are saved, or reuses an existing repository in this folder or a parent. Initialization is optional and creates no commit. The C++ `.gitignore` is generated either way; existing rules are retained after the generated defaults so user exceptions win. The CLI's default is to skip Git initialization.

## Understand the starting choices

Use `--standard 17|20|23|26` to choose the language mode and `--module-layout single|split` for named-module arrangement. C++17 defaults to headers.

Defaults are C++26, named project modules, standard-library headers, an application with a reusable static core library, feature folders, Ninja, and LLVM formatting. The sample partitions a range of work into balanced worker ranges; it does not launch threads or initialize a graphics API.

The project includes a small test executable registered with CTest. CTest runs tests; Catch2 is a separate testing library and is not included. The starter builds without fetching dependencies.

`--modules` chooses how your own interfaces are organized. `--std-modules true` separately enables `import std;` and currently needs verified CMake versions 4.4.0 through 4.4.4 plus a compatible compiler and standard library. Read [Modules and headers](/docs/modules/) before selecting it.

## Keep changes in the project

`.rivet.json` records the project model. Rivet creates files inside the selected project root, refuses existing destination files except for the reviewed `.gitignore` merge, and checks for changes between preview and application. Later operations regenerate its owned CMake fragments while leaving custom build logic in the root `CMakeLists.txt`.

Use [Files and targets](/docs/templates/) to add your first component. Generated `AGENTS.md` explains the conventions to an assistant; Rivet does not contact an AI service or automatically rewrite arbitrary CMake.
